    "bottom_line": "Establishes the definitions a liver surgeon now works to: clinically significant portal hypertension as an HVPG of 10 mmHg or more, ruled out by liver stiffness of 15 kPa or less with platelets of 150 x 10^9/L or more, ruled in at 25 kPa or more, with an ANTICIPATE-handled grey zone between, and it names clinically significant portal hypertension as a risk marker for decompensation and death after resection for HCC. It does not establish a threshold at which resection should be declined, and says so; the thresholds it does give are validated only in viral, alcohol-related and non-obese NASH disease, and in NASH cirrhosis the faculty concede portal hypertension can be clinically evident below the 10 mmHg definition. Use the numbers to describe risk, not to decide operability.",
    "funding": "The participants declared that they have nothing to disclose regarding funding or conflict of interest with respect to the manuscript, while the acknowledgements record that the workshop was endorsed and supported by unrestricted grants from EASL, AASLD and eight further national societies and research networks; separately, three named authors declare consultancy or research support from Zydus, Surrozen, Actelion, AbbVie, Bayer, Boehringer-Ingelheim, Gilead, Intercept, MSD, Philips, Pliant Pharmaceuticals, Roche and Siemens, and one is supported by the Stiftung für Leberkrankheiten"
