    "bottom_line": "Establishes the 2022 version of the staging and treatment algorithm most of the world uses for HCC, adds a liver-function and patient-characterisation layer above tumour burden, and brings radioembolisation in for single nodules up to 8 cm. It does not establish any of this systematically: it is an expert narrative review drafted by two of its eighteen authors, with no search strategy, no voting, no thresholds for the liver-function variables it introduces, and no rule for the treatment stage migration on which the model depends. Use it as the field's shared vocabulary, and read the published correspondence from radiation oncologists, surgeons and hepatologists as part of the document.",
    "funding": "No commercial funding; the authors' research is partially supported by Instituto de Salud Carlos III, the Spanish Health Ministry's National Strategic Plan against Hepatitis C, AECC, CIBERehd, an EASL grant, NIH R01 MD012565 and Horizon 2020 grant agreement 952103. Industry relationships are printed in full and are extensive: the first and last authors each declare consultancy, lecture or travel support from more than ten companies including Bayer, BMS, Roche, Ipsen, AstraZeneca, Eisai, Lilly, Sirtex and Terumo, with institutional research funding from Bayer and Ipsen, while two of the eighteen authors declare nothing"
